===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{senseid|en|explicit or definite}} [[explicit|Explicit]] or [[definite]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ashlyn Barry; Justin A. Haegele; Daniel Schaefer; Kristen A. Pickett; Luis Columna|title=Autistic Young Adults’ Experiences and Recommendations for Strength Training|date=2025-08-20|work=Journal of Autism and Developmental Disorders|url=//link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10803-025-07017-1|doi=10.1007/s10803-025-07017-1|passage=While some participants, like Gabi and Jasmine, had access to a gym but still felt uncertain about using '''specific''' types of equipment, others who trained at home faced even greater challenges due to the absence of both a coach and proper equipment.}}
# {{senseid|en|of a taxonomic species of life}} {{lb|en|bioscience|taxonomy}} Pertaining to a [[species]], as a taxon or taxa at the rank of [[species]].
#: {{hypo|en|monospecific|multispecific|oligospecific|paucispecific}}
#: {{hol|en|generic<id:of whole genera of life>|familial}}
#: {{mer|en|infrasubspecific|infraspecific|subspecific}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=w:Richard Dawkins|title=The Oxford Book of Modern Science Writing|page=3|publisher=Oxford|year_published=2009|passage=Science and literature, then, are the two achievements of ''Homo sapiens'' that most convincingly justify the '''specific''' name.}}
# {{lb|en|sometimes in combination}} [[special|Special]], [[distinctive]] or [[unique]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 26, 2023
|author=Rayna Skiver
|title= Actually Bad for Kids? Here’s What Experts Say
|work=Green Matters
|url= https://www.greenmatters.com/parenting/why-is-cocomelon-bad-for-kids
|page=
|passage= A psychologist told WJLA TV that, for the most part, this isn’t a Cocomelon-'''specific''' issue. The main issue is too much screen time and children's shows with fast-paced sequences.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260905224709/https://www.greenmatters.com/parenting/why-is-cocomelon-bad-for-kids|archivedate=5 September 2026}}
# {{senseid|en|particular thing}} [[intend]]ed for, or [[apply]]ing to, a [[particular]] thing.
# {{senseid|en|identifying something with high accuracy}} Serving to identify a particular thing (often a disease or condition), with little risk of mistaking something else for it.
#: {{ux|en|a highly '''specific''' test}}
#: {{ux|en|'''specific''' and nonspecific symptoms}}
# Being a [[remedy]] for a particular [[disease]] on a deeper level, rather than just masking the symptoms.
#: {{ux|en|Quinine is a '''specific''' medicine in cases of malaria.}}
#: {{ux|en|Any improvement in secondary sciatica is probably due to the analgesic action of the sodium salicylate, but in primary sciatica, in all likelihood “rheumatic,” the effect of the sodium salicylate appears to be '''specific''' rather than symptomatic.}}
#* {{RQ:Coleridge Table Talk|volume=I|chapter=Life|page=147|passage=The study of '''specific''' medicines is too much disregarded now. No doubt, the hunting after specifics is a mark of ignorance and weakness in medicine, yet the neglect of them is proof also of immaturity; for, in fact, all medicines will be found '''specific''' in the perfection of science.}}
# {{senseid|en|immunologically regarding Ab-Ag}} {{lb|en|immunology}} [[limit|Limit]]ed to a particular [[antibody]] or [[antigen]].
# {{senseid|en|by mass, in physics}} {{lb|en|physics}} Of a value divided by mass (e.g. specific orbital energy). 
# {{senseid|en|by any standardizing divisor, in physics}} {{lb|en|physics}} Similarly referring to a value divided by any measure which acts to standardize it (e.g. thrust specific fuel consumption, referring to fuel consumption divided by thrust).
# {{senseid|en|instance of dimensionless ratio, in physics}} {{lb|en|physics}} A measure compared with a standard reference value by division, to produce a ratio [[dimensionless|without unit or dimension]] (e.g. specific [[refractive index]] is a pure number, and is relative to that of air).

